From e25cc7a1ceedb4d28219dfc55984ff89833cd6d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 09:05:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(sop-detector): run the SOP detector inside the backend
 container on AWS

The AWS site only deploys the backend (deploy-backend.yml), so the SOP
detector, which works on the local site, never reached it. Merging this
puts it there without any AWS changes:

- SourceCode/backend/Dockerfile installs the detector beside the backend,
  with its own Python 3.14 virtualenv (its models need the exact library
  versions in its requirements.txt), when the build is given
  --build-context sop-detector=../../SOP-detector. Without it the image is
  the backend alone, as before.
- start.sh starts the detector on 127.0.0.1:8100, again if it stops, and
  then the backend.
- app/routers/sop_detector.py forwards /sop-detector/* to it, with the
  backend's upload limit and API key check; 503 while it is not running.
- deploy-backend.yml passes the folder to the build and also redeploys on
  changes under SOP-detector/. build-backend-image.yml builds and starts
  the image on pull requests, without pushing anything.
- The frontend's production build turns the detector panel on
  (.env.production) and calls /sop-detector on the backend's host, taken
  from VITE_API_BASE_URL, with the API key like the other calls.

The detector shares the backend task's CPUs at a lower priority, so a new
video takes longer than on a machine of its own; videos it has already
read are answered at once.

